\begin{abstract}
We present a unified, fully formalized development of four pillars of extremal
combinatorics, together with two cross-domain bridges and a foundational theory
of graph saturation. We establish \textbf{Tur\'an's theorem} in both an integer
and a real density form---a $K_{r+1}$-free graph on $n$ vertices has at most
$\left(1 - 1/r\right) n^2/2$ edges---and specialize it to \textbf{Mantel's
theorem} ($n^2/4$ for triangle-free graphs). We bridge Mantel's bound to Ramsey
theory: on at least six vertices, a triangle-free graph forces a triangle in its
complement, fusing the extremal and Ramsey viewpoints at $R(3,3) = 6$. We extract
the \textbf{Kruskal--Katona} shadow bound $\binom{k}{r} \le |\mathcal A|
\Rightarrow \binom{k}{r-1} \le |\partial \mathcal A|$, deduce that iterated
shadows of dense uniform families never vanish prematurely, and bridge it to
graphs: a graph with at least $\binom{k}{3}$ triangles has at least $\binom{k}{2}$
edges. We package the \textbf{triangle removal lemma} in textbook,
contrapositive, and dichotomy forms, and use the downstream asymptotics to prove
\textbf{Roth's theorem} on $3$-term arithmetic progressions, both as a density
limit $r_3(N)/N \to 0$ and in the qualitative form ``frequently-dense sets contain
a $3$-AP.'' Finally we build the basic theory of \textbf{saturation numbers},
proving existence of saturated graphs, the inequality $\sat(n,H) \le \ex(n,H)$,
the apex-join edge count, exact edge counts for the matching-plus-isolated family,
and the clique saturation bound $\sat(n, K_{r+1}) \le e(T(n,r))$. We state the
Cameron--Puleo saturation recurrence as a formal conjecture. All results are
proved with no unverified assumptions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Extremal graph theory studies the maximum or minimum amount of local structure
(edges, cliques, set members) a combinatorial object may carry subject to a
forbidden-substructure constraint. The unifying phenomenon is that \emph{abundance
forces structure}: beyond a sharp, computable threshold, a forbidden pattern
becomes unavoidable. This paper develops four cornerstone instances of that
principle and ties them together:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Tur\'an / Mantel}---too many edges force a clique;
  \item \textbf{Kruskal--Katona}---too many uniform sets force a large shadow;
  \item \textbf{Triangle removal (Szemer\'edi regularity)}---robust triangle
        presence forces cubically many triangles;
  \item \textbf{Roth}---too many integers force an arithmetic progression.
\end{enumerate}
We additionally develop the dual \emph{saturation} theory, which minimizes edges
over maximally-cautious graphs.

\paragraph{Notation.} Throughout, $G$ is a finite simple graph on a vertex type
$V$ with $n = |V|$, and $e(G)$ is its number of edges. $G$ is $K_{r+1}$-free if it
contains no $(r+1)$-clique. The Tur\'an graph $T(n,r)$ is the balanced complete
$r$-partite graph on $n$ vertices. For an $r$-uniform family $\mathcal A$,
$\partial \mathcal A$ is its shadow and $\partial^{[i]}\mathcal A$ its $i$-th
iterated shadow. We write $r_3(N)$ for the size of the largest $3$-AP-free subset
of $\{0,\dots,N-1\}$, and $\mathrm{ThreeAPFree}(A)$ for the absence of a nontrivial
$3$-term progression in $A$.

\section{Tur\'an's theorem and Mantel's theorem}

\subsection{The integer edge bound}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{turan\_edge\_bound\_nat}]
If $G$ is $K_{r+1}$-free, then $2r\, e(G) \le (r-1)\, n^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Among all $K_{r+1}$-free graphs on $n$ vertices, $T(n,r)$ is extremal:
$e(G) \le e(T(n,r))$ by \texttt{CliqueFree.card\_edgeFinset\_le}. The exact count
\texttt{card\_edgeFinset\_turanGraph} and the inequality
\texttt{mul\_card\_edgeFinset\_turanGraph\_le} give $2r\, e(T(n,r)) \le (r-1)n^2$;
chaining a monotonicity step closes the goal.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The real density form}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{turan\_edge\_bound\_real}]
If $G$ is $K_{r+1}$-free and $r \ge 1$, then
\[ e(G) \le \left(1 - \frac{1}{r}\right)\frac{n^2}{2}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $r = m+1$ to remove natural-number truncation. Casting the integer bound to
$\RR$ gives $2(m+1)\,e(G) \le m\,n^2$. The identity
$\left(1 - \tfrac{1}{m+1}\right)\tfrac{n^2}{2} = \tfrac{m\,n^2}{2(m+1)}$ rewrites
the goal, and a clearing-of-denominators step finishes.
\end{proof}

This is exactly $\ex(n, K_{r+1}) \le (1 - 1/r)\,n^2/2$.

\subsection{Mantel's theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{mantel\_nat}, \texttt{mantel\_real}]
If $G$ is triangle-free, then $4\,e(G) \le n^2$, equivalently $e(G) \le n^2/4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Instantiate the Tur\'an bounds at $r = 2$ and simplify.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A cross-domain bridge: Tur\'an meets Ramsey}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{mantel\_ramsey\_bridge}]
If $6 \le |V|$ and $G$ is triangle-free, then both $4\,e(G) \le n^2$ and there is
a set $S$ on which the complement $\Gc$ contains a triangle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first conjunct is Mantel. For the second, apply $R(3,3)=6$
(\texttt{arrows\_three\_three}): every $2$-coloring of $K_n$, $n \ge 6$, has a
monochromatic triangle. Reading $G$ as ``red,'' the unavoidable triangle cannot
be red (triangle-freeness), hence is blue, i.e.\ lies in $\Gc$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Kruskal--Katona shadow bound}

\subsection{Single-shadow lower bound}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{kk\_shadow\_lower}]
Let $\mathcal A$ be a family of $r$-subsets of $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ with
$1 \le r \le k \le n$. If $\binom{k}{r} \le |\mathcal A|$, then
$\binom{k}{r-1} \le |\partial \mathcal A|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $i=1$ case of the Lov\'asz form $\binom{k}{r-i} \le |\partial^{[i]}\mathcal A|$
(\texttt{kruskal\_katona\_lovasz\_form}), simplifying $\partial^{[1]} = \partial$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Iterated shadows never vanish prematurely}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{kk\_iterated\_shadow\_nonempty}]
Under the same hypotheses, for every $i \le r$ the shadow
$\partial^{[i]}\mathcal A$ is nonempty.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The Lov\'asz form gives $\binom{k}{r-i} \le |\partial^{[i]}\mathcal A|$. Since
$r-i \le k$, the coefficient $\binom{k}{r-i}$ is positive, so the shadow is
nonempty. The case $i = r$ ($\binom k0 = 1$) shows the chain descends to $\varnothing$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A bridge to graphs: many triangles force many edges}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{triangles\_sized}, \texttt{shadow\_triangles\_subset\_edges}]
The triangle family of $G$ is $3$-uniform, and
$\partial(\text{triangles of }G) \subseteq \text{edges of }G$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every shadow member is $s \setminus \{a\}$ for a triangle $s$ and $a \in s$;
erasing one vertex of a $3$-clique leaves an adjacent pair of cardinality $2$, an
edge.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{card\_cliqueFinset\_two\_ge\_of\_triangles},
\texttt{card\_edgeFinset\_ge\_of\_triangles}]
If $3 \le k \le n$ and a graph has at least $\binom k3$ triangles, then it has at
least $\binom k2$ edges.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the Lov\'asz form at $i=1$, $r=3$ to bound the triangle shadow by
$\binom k2$; transfer the bound to edges via the containment above and the
identity \texttt{card\_cliqueFinset\_two\_eq\_edgeFinset} ($2$-cliques $=$ edges).
\end{proof}

\section{The triangle removal lemma}

Let $\delta(\varepsilon) = \texttt{triangleRemovalBound}(\varepsilon) > 0$.

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{not\_farFromTriangleFree\_of\_few\_triangles}]
If $\#(G\text{'s triangles}) < \delta(\varepsilon)\, n^3$, then $G$ is not
$\varepsilon$-far from triangle-free: deleting fewer than $\varepsilon n^2$ edges
makes it triangle-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $G$ were $\varepsilon$-far, the raw removal lemma yields a triangle-free
$G' \le G$ with $e(G) - e(G') < \varepsilon n^2$, while
\texttt{FarFromTriangleFree.le\_card\_sub\_card} forces the reverse inequality---a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{triangle\_removal\_lemma}]
For every $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $\delta > 0$ such that every finite graph $H$
with fewer than $\delta\, n^3$ triangles has a subgraph $H' \le H$ that is
triangle-free with $e(H) - e(H') < \varepsilon n^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{triangle\_count\_dichotomy}]
For any $\varepsilon$, either $\delta(\varepsilon)\, n^3 \le \#(G\text{'s
triangles})$, or $G$ can be made triangle-free by deleting fewer than
$\varepsilon n^2$ edges.
\end{theorem}

Informally: \emph{either a graph has cubically many triangles, or it is edge-close
to triangle-free}---there is no middle ground.

\section{Roth's theorem on 3-term arithmetic progressions}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{rothNumberNat\_density\_tendsto\_zero}]
$\displaystyle \frac{r_3(N)}{N} \to 0$ as $N \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{exists\_threeAP\_of\_freq\_dense}]
Let $A \subseteq \NN$ and $c > 0$. If there are infinitely many $N$ with
$c\,N \le \#\{n < N : n \in A\}$, then $A$ contains a nontrivial $3$-term
arithmetic progression ($a + c = 2b$, $a \ne b$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Assume $A$ is $3$-AP-free. From $r_3 = o(N)$, at $\varepsilon = c/2$ we get an
eventual upper bound $r_3(N) \le (c/2)N$. Combining the frequent lower bound with
this eventual upper bound at a single $N \ge 1$, the window
$B = A \cap \{0,\dots,N-1\}$ is $3$-AP-free, so
$cN \le |B| \le r_3(N) \le (c/2)N$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Saturation theory}

\begin{definition}
$\mathrm{edgeCount}(G) = |G.\mathtt{edgeSet}|$. $G$ is \emph{$H$-saturated} if $H$
does not embed in $G$ but adding any missing edge creates a copy of $H$.
$\ex(n,H)$ is the maximum edge count of an $H$-free graph on $\mathrm{Fin}\,n$;
$\sat(n,H)$ is the minimum edge count of an $H$-saturated graph.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{exists\_isSaturated}]
If $H$ has an edge, then an $H$-saturated graph exists on $\mathrm{Fin}\,n$ for
every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A maximum-edge $H$-free graph is saturated: adding any edge while staying free
would contradict maximality (\texttt{edgeCount\_lt\_addEdge}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{satNum\_le\_exNum}]
If $H$ has an edge, then $\sat(n,H) \le \ex(n,H)$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{cone} $K_1 \vee H$ adds an apex vertex adjacent to all of $H$. The graph
$tK_2 \cup qK_1$ (\texttt{matchingPlusIsolated}) is a matching of $t$ edges plus
$q$ isolated vertices on $\mathrm{Fin}(2t+q)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{edgeCount\_cone},
\texttt{edgeCount\_matchingPlusIsolated},
\texttt{edgeCount\_cone\_matchingPlusIsolated}]
$e(K_1 \vee H) = |V| + e(H)$;\quad $e(tK_2 \cup qK_1) = t$;\quad
$e\big(K_1 \vee (tK_2 \cup qK_1)\big) = (2t+q) + t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{satNum\_clique\_le\_turan}]
For $r \ge 1$ and every $n$, $\sat(n, K_{r+1}) \le e(T(n,r))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply $\sat \le \ex$ to $H = K_{r+1}$, then bound $\ex$ by the Tur\'an edge count
via \texttt{CliqueFree.card\_edgeFinset\_le} and
\texttt{card\_edgeFinset\_turanGraph}.
\end{proof}

\begin{conjecture}[\texttt{CameronPuleoEquality}]
For $t \ge 1$, $q \ge 1$, $n > 2t+q$,
\[ \sat\big(n,\ K_1 \vee (tK_2 \cup qK_1)\big) = (n-1) + \sat\big(n-1,\ tK_2 \cup qK_1\big). \]
Stated as a \texttt{Prop}; proved for $t = 1, 2$ in the source paper, open in
general.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Discussion and future work}

Across all four pillars the same engine runs: \emph{abundance forces structure}.
Too many edges force a clique (Tur\'an/Mantel); too many uniform sets force a large
shadow (Kruskal--Katona); robustly hard-to-remove triangles force $\Theta(n^3)$
triangles (removal); too many integers force a $3$-AP (Roth). Concrete
continuations include: matching the Tur\'an upper bound with a multipartite lower
bound for exact equality; summing the Lov\'asz shadow layers $\binom{k}{r-i}$ into
a full Pascal-triangle total; an explicit additive-energy dichotomy for $3$-AP
density; and settling the Cameron--Puleo recurrence beyond $t = 1, 2$.

\section{Conclusion}

We have assembled a coherent, fully verified tour of extremal combinatorics:
Tur\'an and Mantel edge bounds, the Kruskal--Katona shadow inequality and its
graph incarnation, the triangle removal dichotomy, Roth's theorem in density and
qualitative forms, and the foundational theory of saturation numbers with a Tur\'an
bridge. The recurring moral---that beyond a sharp threshold, structure is
unavoidable---is realized as a small number of interlocking, machine-checked
theorems.

\end{document}

